# id: M34-append-from-zero-over-a-snapshot
# expect: killed
# covering-test: TestASnapshottedPrefixRefusesAnAppendFromZero
# package: ./raft/
# power-covered-by: TestASnapshottedPrefixRefusesAnAppendFromZero -- a DIRECTED test, and the exemption is earned by measurement rather than by the sentence. The PRECONDITION is an append from index 1 carrying entries arriving at a node whose prefix is ALREADY in a snapshot, and counted with the ordering rather than assumed it reads: A7 shape 1 in 200 seeds (5,051 such appends, one meeting the ordering, at seed 155); a3 0 of 200; a2 0 of 200. The previous declaration floored this class under a2 -- a shape in which the precondition does not occur at all -- and cited 2 of 3000 at commit A5-close, before the term-start no-op moved every trace. Re-measured at HEAD: 0 of 3000 under a2. The class did not become safe and it did not become unreachable; the floor was pointed at a shape that cannot produce it. The directed test gives the node a snapshot itself and cannot drift, and it kills this patch by the original symptom: a panic from truncateFrom, "an entry this node was told was committed is being overwritten"
# power-refutation: the sweep is not the instrument, and the previous covering test proves the point rather than the class -- TestSnapshotPrefixIsNotOverwritten is a 3,000-seed A2 sweep that PASSES with this patch applied at HEAD, so the mutant survived its own covering test for a phase. A covering test built on a sweep stops covering when the workload moves, and it does it silently
# mutates: a node whose applied prefix is already in a snapshot accepts an append starting at index 1, because the consistency check treats PrevLogIndex 0 as agreeable to everybody. It then overwrites entries it has already applied and told the cluster were committed
# re-anchored: 2026-08-27, tools/anchorcheck. Context regenerated so the hunk anchors on CODE
#   rather than on prose. The mutation is unchanged and was proved so: the old patch and this
#   one were applied to clean copies and the results are byte-identical.
--- a/raft/raft.go
+++ b/raft/raft.go
@@ -3029,33 +3029,33 @@ func (r *Raft) matches(idx Index, term Term) bool {
 	if idx == 0 {
 		// "Append from the very beginning" is only agreeable to a node that HAS
 		// a beginning. Once a prefix is in a snapshot, accepting an append at
 		// index 1 means overwriting entries this node has already applied --
 		// and it is not a hypothetical: a leader whose view of this follower has
 		// been reset sends exactly this, and the follower's own commit index is
 		// already past it.
 		//
 		// Found at 10k by the assertion BUG-007 corrected: "node 3 truncated to
 		// 1 with commit index 6". The old form of that assertion, which fired on
 		// the durable watermark instead of the commit index, would have caught a
 		// legal truncation here and missed this one.
 		//
 		// Rejecting is safe and self-correcting: the reject carries lastIndex as
 		// its hint, so the leader jumps forward, finds the follower is past its
 		// own log, and sends a snapshot.
-		return r.snapIndex == 0
+		return true
 	}
 	if idx > r.lastIndex() {
 		return false
 	}
 	if idx < r.snapIndex {
 		// The entry is behind our snapshot: part of a prefix already applied,
 		// which this node cannot re-verify and must not pretend to. Refusing is
 		// safe -- the reject carries lastIndex as its hint, so a leader that is
 		// this far behind our state jumps forward in one round rather than
 		// walking backwards into a log that no longer exists.
 		return false
 	}
 	return r.termAt(idx) == term
 }
 
